<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Albarracín</h1><p class="st-meta">1170 CE – 1284 CE · capital Albarracín</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A tiny independent Christian lordship in the mountains of Teruel, whose Azagra lords refused to be vassals of either Castile or Aragon.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1170 the Navarrese noble <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-ruiz-de-azagra">Pedro Ruiz de Azagra</a> obtained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albarracin">Albarracín</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-mardanish">Ibn Mardanish</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-taifa-of-murcia">Second Taifa of Murcia</a>, whom he had served. He resettled the town with Christians and declared that he held it as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a> alone.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albarracin">Albarracín</a> ruled a mountain territory of pasture and forest, granted a fuero to settlers and created a bishopric. Their autonomy rested on balancing between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon</a>, and the family kept it for over a century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The lordship passed by marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-nunez-i-de-lara">Juan Núñez de Lara</a>. In 1284 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iii-of-aragon">Peter III of Aragon</a> besieged and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albarracin">Albarracín</a>, and it was incorporated into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon</a> in 1300.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 1170 CE – 1284 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Iberia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:82.99%;width:2.27%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1170 CE – 1284 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Albarracín</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pedro Ruiz de Azagra, Juan Núñez de Lara</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Aragonese, Castilian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Señorío de Albarracín</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1227&amp;focus=lordship-of-albarracin" aria-label="Lordship of Albarracín on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4645 734 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Albarracín lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4965 990L4965 990M4965 990L4984 945"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4965 990h0M4984 945h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4965 990h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4965 990h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1227&amp;focus=lordship-of-albarracin">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
